prompts.intake:
#! AXIOM/2.0 name=source_placement_intake
@PURPOSE: Perform the inference needed to refactor an AUE before assigning implementation work. Connect the existing functional map to the code that realizes its encounters. Preserve the reasoning, evidence and intended changes at the RE so subsequent agents can retrieve and apply that understanding.
@STATE_INIT: {Prompt} := optional JSON text in the Refactor input block following this procedure {Scope} := the functional Addresses and source files implicated by {Prompt}; otherwise the current map {Layer} := source_placement {Base} := the active base inspected for this intake {Evidence} := source revision or file hashes, graph generation, coverage and the queries actually used {Unresolved} := []
@SEMANTICS: Read metaform://glossary and intake_schema. Apply their definitions as written. Work from each RE's OP and the PE's EF for that encounter. Retain IFD as the map defines it. The same ordinary element can be RE in one purpose record and PE in another. Follow this form at every depth. Source symbols identify implementation. Keep their file and qualified name separate from functional Addresses. One element may use several symbols; one symbol may serve several purposes, each with its own reason. A call edge is implementation evidence. Its direction does not determine IFD or semantic ownership. Refactoring knowledge belongs to the RE in {Layer}; exact implementation links belong in the code layer. Keep source observations, inferred associations and proposed changes distinguishable, with their reasons.
@EXECUTE_PIPELINE(Orient): [1] Read the active base and layers with map_bases and map_layers. Resolve {Scope} against the map and the actual artifacts. Read the project instructions and honor its excluded paths. [2] Read each receiving element and its purposes with map_where. Read the code and applicable specifications before inferring a boundary. If the functional map is absent, report the intake needed to establish it. [3] If {Layer} exists, inspect and extend its relevant items. Otherwise create it: map_knowledge(op="create", layer="source_placement", module="source_placement", mutable=true, hideable=true, variable="The code serving each receiving element's purpose and the inferred context needed to refactor that code while preserving its required behavior.", sources=[the inspected base layer name]). Record the procedure revision, scope and evidence with the work. Retain existing records when resuming. => EXECUTE_PIPELINE(Structure)
@EXECUTE_PIPELINE(Structure): [1] Inspect the connected code-graph server's project and schema. For codebase-memory use list_projects, get_graph_schema and check_index_coverage for the evidence paths. Read source for stale, missing or unresolved coverage. Record what the graph can and cannot establish. [2] Use Cypher for bounded evidence retrieval. Use map_cypher for the functional map, and the code-graph server's query_graph for symbols and implementation relationships. Their schemas are different. The functional map's direct RE-to-PE edges have kind="join" and retain purpose, ef and ifd: MATCH (re)-[r]->(pe) WHERE r.kind = "join" RETURN re.address, r.purpose, r.ef, r.ifd, pe.address LIMIT 200 Narrow queries to the current scope and follow returned pages. Retrieve isolated symbols as well as edges. On the code graph, query the relationships its schema actually exposes, including calls, callback references, state uses/writes, imports and tests where available. Save the actual queries. [3] Import or refresh the code layer with map_code_import using the available provider. For the connected codebase-memory provider use from="mcp". Read map_code_gaps and map_code_context for this scope. A source-file citation may have linked every declaration in that file. Examine those unreviewed links rather than treating a shared file or line range as proof that every symbol serves every cited element. Follow pagination and reported additional counts; use scoped searches when a context read is truncated. [4] Read source around the candidate symbols and their registration sites. Capture callbacks, event names, state ownership and lifecycle, asynchronous ordering, external interfaces and relevant tests. A missing graph edge remains unknown until examined; a zero-caller function can still be an event handler. => EXECUTE_PIPELINE(Analyze)
@EXECUTE_PIPELINE(Analyze): [1] Use NetworkX on the retrieved evidence. Keep the functional graph and implementation graph distinct. Build a MultiDiGraph keyed by (file, qualified_name) for implementation symbols, retaining each edge's kind and evidence. Include isolated symbols. Build a directed call-dependency projection for analysis. [2] Compute strongly_connected_components to find mutually dependent groups, and condensation to examine dependencies between those groups. Inspect incoming/outgoing edges across candidate boundaries, shared state and reachability from known entry points. Self-recursion and incomplete graph coverage must remain distinguishable from dependencies between separate responsibilities. NetworkX sketch over retrieved symbols and call edges: import networkx as nx calls = nx.DiGraph() calls.add_nodes_from(symbol_keys) calls.add_edges_from(call_edges) groups = list(nx.strongly_connected_components(calls)) condensed = nx.condensation(calls, groups) Caller-to-callee order is not automatically an implementation sequence. Reason about the functional requirements, state lifecycle and preserved interfaces before deciding what can move together. [3] Reconcile structural evidence with each RE's OP. Use map_code_search and source reads to identify the narrowest functional element served. Record exact symbols through map_code_link, with the why. For a specific encounter, node=<RE address> and provider=<PE address> identify the purpose served. The existing workflow skill code_links can perform this association pass; its proposals remain proposals until accepted through its existing lifecycle. Keep any unresolved associations explicit. => EXECUTE_PIPELINE(Record)
@EXECUTE_PIPELINE(Record): [1] Record each useful inference when resolved, as an item of {Layer} at its receiving element: map_knowledge(op="add-item", layer="source_placement", node="element:<RE address>", value=<structured knowledge>, refs=[["path", <source path>], ["symbol", <qualified name>], ...]). Include a short title and why in the value. For an encounter-specific item, identify its receiver and provider in the value, keeping the item at the RE. Read the existing items before adding or updating one; use map_knowledge(op="edit", layer="source_placement", id=<existing item id>, value=<updated knowledge>) for the same inference, keeping unrelated items intact. [2] Record the information that makes the contemplated work actionable: - exact implementation symbols and how they serve the recorded purpose; - entry points, callers, callees, event/callback wiring and external contracts implicated by the change; - state owners, lifetime, side effects and ordering that affect the proposed boundary; - required behavior to preserve and the evidence establishing it; - the proposed change boundary, dependencies between work assignments and the reason for the sequence; - existing verification commands/tests, what each establishes, and checks still needed; - unresolved questions, their affected decisions, and the evidence needed to resolve them; - source revision or hashes and graph coverage so later changes can identify stale reasoning. Shape each record around its RE and purpose. Omit inapplicable fields rather than manufacturing content. Keep existing faults separate from behavior that must be preserved. A graph score alone decides neither. [3] The procedure prepares the refactor. Describe contemplated source changes as proposals; implementation follows the resulting assignments. Keep reusable inference in the layer instead of base note fields. => EXECUTE_PIPELINE(Proof)
@EXECUTE_PIPELINE(Proof): [1] Read the recorded knowledge back with map_where address=<RE address>@source_placement and the code associations with map_code_context address=<address>. For a building layer, use map_node with raw=true to inspect its items before finishing it. Check each addressed purpose against its symbols and source evidence. [2] Account for the requested scope: prepared responsibilities, shared dependencies, unplaced symbols, missing behavior and unresolved evidence. Make gaps explicit rather than assigning code by proximity. [3] Finish the source_placement layer with map_knowledge(op="finish", layer="source_placement") when the pass is recorded. Report the prepared Addresses and how an editing agent reads each assignment and its evidence. Subsequent work refreshes the code evidence and reexamines affected inference when code changes. => YIELD the source_placement layer and its scope, prepared work, verification and unresolved decisions.
